Appraisal is your read of what’s happening and what it means for you. Projection is when something you feel, want, or intend gets attributed to someone else. Projection can happen inside an appraisal.

Say someone gives you a short reply and walks off.

Thought	What it tells you
“They said ‘fine’ and walked off.”	What you observed.
“That felt dismissive.”	Your appraisal of the interaction.
“They’re angry with me.”	A hypothesis about their inner state.
“I’m angry with them, and I may be reading that anger as theirs.”	A possible projection.

That last distinction matters. You can misread somebody because you’re missing context, because they’re hard to read, or because you’ve seen a similar situation before. Projection is one possible source of a misread. And you can be angry at someone who really is angry with you. Knowing your own state helps you weigh the evidence.

The best real-time test is “What came from them, what came from me, and what would change my mind?” If the evidence is a single short reply, keep the conclusion loose. If they’ve repeatedly brushed off the same concern and tell you directly they’re upset, your appraisal has more to stand on. You can also act on the part you know. “I wanted to finish that conversation. Can we come back to it?” works whether they were angry, distracted, or just late.

It’s the half-painted fence again. The bare patch is real. What it means is an inference. The AI saw where she stopped and supplied a rule about how the whole fence should look. We do something similar when we sense tension and supply a story about whose tension it is. The feeling is real. The next question is what it’s evidence of.